WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.312
Now we will run our first query Before clicking Run
read the

00:00:04.338 --> 00:00:09.138
statement from top to bottom The SELECT clause lists
six columns patient

00:00:09.162 --> 00:00:14.700
ID MRN full name age gender and active status That
means those

00:00:14.725 --> 00:00:18.700
six fields will appear in the result The FROM clause
says that

00:00:18.725 --> 00:00:23.000
the data comes from the patients table The final
clause ORDER BY

00:00:23.025 --> 00:00:28.250
patient ID sorts the result using the patient
identifier Notice that there

00:00:28.275 --> 00:00:32.912
is no WHERE clause Because we did not add a filter
PostgreSQL

00:00:32.938 --> 00:00:37.012
can return all rows from the patients table We are
controlling the

00:00:37.038 --> 00:00:40.362
columns and the order but not removing any rows
based on a

00:00:40.388 --> 00:00:45.675
condition Click Run and look at the output Compare
the column headings

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:49.888
with the SELECT list Then look at the order of
patient IDs

00:00:50.775 --> 00:00:55.100
The results should follow the ordering requested by
the query This is

00:00:55.125 --> 00:01:00.300
a good moment to separate three ideas SELECT
controls what columns appear

00:01:01.188 --> 00:01:07.512
FROM identifies the source table ORDER BY controls
the presentation order None

00:01:07.538 --> 00:01:11.500
of these clauses changes the stored patient data If
you are new

00:01:11.512 --> 00:01:14.925
to SQL try reading the query aloud in plain language
show these

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.088
patient fields from the patients table and sort the
rows by patient

00:01:18.112 --> 00:01:23.100
ID Translating SQL into a normal sentence is a very
useful beginner

00:01:23.112 --> 00:01:23.662
technique
